WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:11.630
So our plan today is to talk about what quantum
computers are.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.330
How people are building them.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:15.330
What they can do.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.330
What they can't do.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:19.730
They're not all powerful god like devices
so they do have limitations that we'll get

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:20.910
into as well.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:21.910
OK.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.840
Without further ado let me introduce our esteemed
panelists.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:32.230
Our first panelist is a mathematician, computer
scientist, physicist, expert on quantum information

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:33.230
theory.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:37.940
She was a professor at Tel Aviv University
and a researcher at the CNRS in Paris.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.890
Pease welcome Julia Kempe.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:48.160
Our next participant is the professor of quantum
mechanical engineering, that's a thing, at

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.179
MIT.

00:00:49.179 --> 00:00:53.289
Director of the Keck center for extreme quantum
information theory and hopefully will explain

00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:55.480
why it's extreme to us.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:01.609
Please welcome Seth Lloyd.

00:01:01.609 --> 00:01:06.580
Next up, our next guest comes to us from the
Air Force Research Laboratory in Rome in upstate

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:07.580
New York.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:12.470
She's a senior research scientist there and
the primary investigator for the Trapped-Ion

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.170
Quantum Networking Group.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:18.500
Please welcome Kathy-Anne Soderbergh.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:26.750
And finally coming to us from IBM in Yorktown
Heights, just north of the city, is the Manager

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:31.979
of the Experimental Quantum Computing Group,
distinguished research staff member.

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:38.530
Please welcome Jerry Chow.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.700
So Seth, let me turn to you first.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.580
Quantum mechanics: weird or just unfamiliar?

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:48.610
Definitely weird.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:51.010
It's, I prefer the word funky actually.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.060
OK you heard its.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.580
This is the official terminology.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.590
It's the James Brown of sciences.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:58.590
How so?

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:02.619
I mean why and particularly in terms of computation
what is it?

00:02:02.619 --> 00:02:04.770
What's the special quality of quantum physics?

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:10.690
So in quantum mechanics, things that we think
of being like particles, like basketballs

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:14.330
or soccer balls, have waves that are attached
to them.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:18.569
And so you know I have a ball over here and
it's got a wave and then I have a ball over

00:02:18.569 --> 00:02:20.040
here and it's got a wave.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.060
But the funky thing about that is that the
waves can add up so I can have a ball that's

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.850
both here and there at the same time.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:32.040
And if you map this to a bit in a quantum
computer, so this is zero, ball over here,

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:36.840
and ball over there is one, then I can have
a quantum bit or qubit that is zero and one

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:37.840
at the same time.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.930
So Julia, why would that be something you
would actually want?

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:46.810
Why would this, I want to actually comment
on the weird first if I may.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:47.810
Funky please.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:48.810
Funky.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.080
Because I think it becomes a, it depends on
your point of view coming at it as a physicist

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.460
with a lot of training in classical physics
it's indeed probably very weird, but if you

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:01.010
look at it as a computer scientist it maybe
becomes less weird because we are not spoiled.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:05.730
Our intuition is still, that it's not, how
can I say, biased in any way.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:11.379
And I view it as something which is like probability
theory except the probabilities can be negative

00:03:11.379 --> 00:03:14.600
or they can be even complex that that is not
so essential.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:18.170
And so in that sense it's not very weird.

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:23.230
It's just gets, requires a bit of getting
used to but it's pretty natural.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:30.840
And so for the qubit, why is it useful to
have qubits in being both zero and one at

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:31.840
the same time?

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:37.519
Cause if we have many of those, we can think
of having various states at the same time

00:03:37.519 --> 00:03:41.939
and we can think of computing all these possibilities
at the same time.

00:03:41.939 --> 00:03:44.849
So that leads us to this massive what's called
quantum parallelism.

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:50.799
So Jerry, can you walk me through like just
a quick numeric example, you have a certain

00:03:50.799 --> 00:03:53.159
number of qubits, what that means in terms
of parallelism?

00:03:53.159 --> 00:03:54.159
Yeah.

00:03:54.159 --> 00:03:59.470
So I mean one of the, as has been mentioned
with regard to these qubits, you have these

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:00.470
superpositions.

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:06.210
And so you might have some number of bits
but instead of bits now you have qubits.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.210
Right.

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:12.170
And in terms of this parallelism what you
can actually have is access to a much larger

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:13.840
space of possibilities.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.340
So if you have n qubits you actually have,
using these principles of superposition and

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:23.380
entanglement, you have access to a space of
up to two to the n possibilities.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:29.720
And so that type of, that type of exponential
space gets really, really large for rather

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.920
modest numbers of qubits.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:37.870
In fact if you get to around n of three hundred
cubits you actually have some state-space

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:41.830
that is greater than the total number of particles
in our universe.

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:47.620
Kathy-Anne, walk me through an extremely simple
numerical example…suppose I, in fact we

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:51.710
do have, but to take an example two of these
qubits.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:54.630
What does that mean in terms of the computational
space that we can work with?

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:58.960
So if you have two qubits you have a four
qubit state space because you can have each

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:05.199
one stored as a zero plus one and the other
stored as a zero plus one, so you get 00011011.

00:05:05.199 --> 00:05:07.610
Whereas classically you can only have zero
or one.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:08.770
You can't have anything in between.

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:12.090
So the computer's in a sense an all possible
computational state space.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.720
Yes that's right.

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:17.160
Now Seth, on the face of it that doesn't sound
like such a good idea because you want to

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.530
get an answer from the computer and it's just
basically telling you everything.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:23.540
Yeah and indeed it's kind of dangerous.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:28.150
If I have a quantum bit that's zero and one
at the same time and I say yo are you zero

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:29.610
or are you one?

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:34.289
Well you know you could, OK, so the electron
is over here and I bring up a very sensitive

00:05:34.289 --> 00:05:37.350
electrometer that says, yo, are you here or
there?

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.900
Well it's either going to show up here, say
with fifty percent probability or there were

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:42.560
fifty percent probability.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.220
So it's just going to behave like something
that's generating a random number which is

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:50.800
kind of useful but it's not, if you actually
want certainty for your answers that's not

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.720
so great.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:55.690
So the kind of the way that these quantum
computations work is you set up all these

00:05:55.690 --> 00:06:01.330
waves and they're wiggling on top of each
other and they're, they're performing multiple

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:02.770
computations simultaneously.

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:09.080
So you can think of an individual wave, a
wave of say electron here and not there, that's

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.669
kind of like a pure tone like ahh.

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:14.819
An electron here and not there is like ahh.

00:06:14.819 --> 00:06:18.560
An electron here and there at the same time
is does somebody want to supply the other

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:19.560
tone?

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:22.000
It's a chord.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.210
So it's you, you get the computational power
from the interference from the kind of symphonic

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.470
nature of this.

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:31.759
So the idea of a quantum computation: you
set up all these waves, they make this beautiful

00:06:31.759 --> 00:06:33.780
music together in this symphonic way.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:38.130
But at the end of the day you actually want
to have an answer that says yes or no, or

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:44.440
zero or one, and all the trickery and talent
goes into making that happen.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:50.740
So Julia, is that, any problem I care to pull
out of a hat or that my professors give me

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:52.180
as a homework assignment.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:58.730
Can I turn it into a problem that's amenable
to this kind of chord pattern that then reduces

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:00.460
to a single pure tone?

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:05.479
That might depend on your artistry but in
general quantum computers are good at certain

00:07:05.479 --> 00:07:11.610
things and we would leave, you know, a lot
of other things to our normal classical computer.

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:18.229
And of course you probably all heard of the
problem that a quantum computer can solve

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:19.560
very well.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:25.099
And that's factoring numbers, large, very
large numbers into primes.

00:07:25.099 --> 00:07:29.060
And the way it's done, just like Seth was
saying and I was saying, you can think of

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:33.979
these amplitudes of a quantum computer as
positive and negative numbers.

00:07:33.979 --> 00:07:40.330
We try to arrange these waves in a way that
all the bad answers cancel out because you

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:44.199
can, you know ,you can have several ways to
arrive at the wrong answer.

00:07:44.199 --> 00:07:49.039
And what you'll try is have some with a positive
amplitude and some with a negative amplitude

00:07:49.039 --> 00:07:53.510
and they cancel out and then you arrive at
the right answer and you, voila you get a

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:56.210
factor for a very big number.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:08:00.940
And that's one of the first and most remarkable
things that was discovered a quantum computer

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:02.230
can do.

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:06.610
And I can elaborate on why it's important.

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:11.710
Most of you, all of you, I assume, are using
credit cards at the machine or over the Internet

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:16.790
and you rely on the fact that they're encryption,
that they are encrypted.

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:24.849
And it so happens that the modern day encryption
is based on assumptions of hardness of factoring

00:08:24.849 --> 00:08:27.030
or problems of that type.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:31.449
And it's these type of problems that a quantum
computer would be able to easily break.

00:08:31.449 --> 00:08:32.449
Interesting.

00:08:32.449 --> 00:08:38.350
Kathy, let me ask as you to drill down a little
bit more here.

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:39.350
Sure

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:46.620
So when I think of a computer I think of it
has, does arithmetic logical operations.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:50.780
How do you, I mean without going into details,
we'll get to some of the details later, but

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:55.860
does the quantum computer also have those
same elements of it can add, it can subtract,

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:58.880
it can do logical comparisons of things?

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:04.531
Yes, yes you need all of the same similar
components to a classical computer to do quantum

00:09:04.531 --> 00:09:05.640
computing.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:10.710
But the way that you make the gates looks
very different than in a conventional computer.

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.270
And that somewhat depends on the underlying
qubit technology.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:15.220
OK I'm going to definitely come back to the
details on that later.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:18.210
I just want to establish that it's a computer.

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:19.210
Yes

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:22.850
It has, you look at it and though it's configured
differently, does have the recognizable qualities

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:23.850
of a computer.

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:24.850
Yes

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:28.770
You can program it in C or Java, and it has
one additional instruction which says take

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:32.280
this quantum bit and put it here and there
at the same time, put it in the state of zero

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:33.420
and one at the same time.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:37.500
So you supplemented ordinary computer language
with just one additional quantum instruction

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:39.760
and you're good to go.

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:41.440
If you can build the thing of course.

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:46.600
Which we'll see in a little bit.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:51.330
Jerry, what are we up to just in general state
of the art about how many qubits we have and

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:54.340
what does that mean in terms of vis a vis
a classical machine.

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:55.340
Yeah.

00:09:55.340 --> 00:10:00.550
So there's many different physical implementations
of building a quantum computer.

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:03.970
The underlying core of a quantum computer
we call a quantum processor.

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:09.060
And what you basically need to build the quantum
processor is something that follows the laws

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:13.900
of quantum mechanics and can have this quantum
mechanical zero and one.

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:19.980
And in terms of where we are in experiments
we're looking at building universal based

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:24.360
quantum computers of order of ten to twenty
qubits at the moment.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:28.560
And that's, that's kind of where the state
of the art is in the field.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:34.340
And just for reference sake, twenty bits doesn't
sound like a lot actually, but how does that

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:37.380
what kind of power does that endow a machine
with?

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:43.220
Well it's actually very interesting because
although you might have twenty qubits you

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:47.750
can actually then have the state space up
to two to the twenty possibilities, right?

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:52.950
But how much you're actually able to access
that then determines, is determined by your

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:53.980
coherence time.

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:58.660
So there's a metric known as coherence time
which says how good of a quantum state can

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:01.080
you actually keep in your quantum processor.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:06.160
And different types of technologies varying
from superconducting to trapped-ions, like

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:09.420
the kind Kathy-Anne works on, have different
amounts of coherence time.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:16.680
So overall this type of time times the number
of qubits, we try to we try to at least my

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:20.170
colleagues that we've only started thinking
about a metric for this called quantum volume

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:23.730
to kind of describe what is the power of a
quantum computer.

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:24.800
So walk me through.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:26.570
What's a quantum volume?

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:30.880
You can basically think of it as how many
steps of these logical operations, or these,

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:36.851
these gate operations that you can do the
superposition or entanglement steps in the

00:11:36.851 --> 00:11:42.060
amount of time that before all the quantum
information is gone, becomes just classical.

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:45.420
And so you have a certain number of steps
and then you have a certain amount of depth

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:50.280
in terms of the total number of qubits that
are connected to one another.

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:53.610
So Julia, let me ask this of you.

00:11:53.610 --> 00:12:00.540
If I just have a phone, a classical ordinary
computer and I store information in it, one

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:03.410
hopes at least it will be able to retain that
for a long period of time.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:05.960
Is Jerry saying that actually it decays away?

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:08.420
And why would it do that inside a quantum
computer?

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:14.620
So the big challenge for a quantum computer
is indeed to maintain these coherences or

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:20.000
these waves that are spread over, not just
you know one qubit but over a collection of,

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.390
in this case, perhaps twenty qubits.

00:12:22.390 --> 00:12:29.070
And what we call entanglement these, these
correlations at a distance of these qubits.

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:33.750
And it is true that this is what nature does
on a very small scale when we describe electrons

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:35.080
and so on.

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:37.650
But it's also true that we don't observe this
in our everyday life.

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:42.300
I mean when you have a bit you have a big
it's either zero or one and the reason we

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:50.380
don't observe it is that once we start interacting
with the environment, once this very fragile

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:55.550
superposition is being subject to the surroundings,
it's being subjected to noise.

00:12:55.550 --> 00:13:01.320
And these very fragile superpositions will
start to what we call decohere, so just disappear.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:06.240
And the point is of course that we need to
be able to address this quantum computer.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:07.300
We need to be able to talk to it.

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:08.940
We need to be able to manipulate it.

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:13.760
So it has to be exposed to us, to our you
know to the world in that sense.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:18.980
And so we're living in this tradeoff situation
where on one hand we need to protect the state.

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:21.330
So we would like to just put it in fridge
and never touch it.

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:24.230
On the other hand we have to touch it in order
to manipulate it.

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:28.360
And this is the big challenge that, you know,
these experimentalists are facing.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:33.460
To battle this decoherence invariably comes
along with the fact that we're exposed to

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:35.350
the, you know, to the environment.

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:39.990
Kathy-Anne, walking into your laboratory,
what would we see and then walk me through

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:40.990
what that represents.

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:41.990
Sure.

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:45.000
So I'll talk about trapped-ion technology
which is what we work on and I believe Jerry

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:48.050
will talk about superconducting qubits in
a few minutes.

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:51.800
They're very different technologies but they're
both very advanced right now in the field.

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:58.200
So for ions we track single atoms and we hold
them, they're they're charged so you can hold

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:00.040
them using electric fields.

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:05.020
So first we prepare a vacuum chamber, because
as you just heard these systems are very fragile,

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:07.740
so you need to protect them from the environment.

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:11.790
So these trapped-ions operate at room temperature
but we hold them in a vacuum chamber roughly

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:15.550
ten to the minus twelve Torr - it's the same
vacuum as outer space.

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:18.560
So the only thing in there is the atoms that
you want to manipulate.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:22.660
And you have a neutral atom source which is
just a piece of metal in a stainless steel

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:29.100
oven that you heat up and then that creates
a beam of neutral atoms which you can put

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:33.170
in your, what's called an ion trap, which
is just a collection of metal electrodes.

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:36.440
Because as I said we trap these using electric
fields since they're charged.

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:40.070
So you put an oscillating electric field on
that trap.

00:14:40.070 --> 00:14:43.050
What that looks like to the atom is a rotating
saddle.

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:44.620
So then that looks like a bowl.

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:48.150
And if you drop a marble in a bowl eventually
it'll come to rest at the bottom of the bowl.

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:51.360
The trapped ions do the exact same thing and
these potentials.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:56.650
And so we shine the neutral atom beam near
the trap and then we have a laser that actually

00:14:56.650 --> 00:15:00.410
rips one of the electrons off the neutral
atom and that makes our ion.

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:01.540
So that leaves it charged.

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:05.620
And at the same time we have to shine a different
color of light in, because coming straight

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:09.580
out of an oven the atoms are essentially screaming
hot and the trap potential just can't catch

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:11.420
an atom it's going that fast.

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:16.230
So you have to cool it down a little bit with
a laser, its laser cooling.

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:20.690
And then that allows you to trap it in this
bowl like potential and then we shine yet

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:26.990
another color of light on the atom and all
these different colors of light create different

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:28.600
transitions within the atomic structure.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:32.630
So if you could look in the atom you would
see different energy levels inside and each

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:35.680
color of light is resonant with a different
energy level.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:37.890
And so the detection light when we shine it
on.

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:42.380
It hits a very strong transition in the atom,
which excites it from it its ground state,

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:45.800
so the qubits themselves are held in the ground
states of these atoms.

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:47.420
There's two ground states.

00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:49.280
And it sends it to an excited state.

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:50.750
It's a very short lived state.

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:54.740
And when it emits it emits a photon and it
does sense that this strong transition it

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:59.310
does that hundreds of thousands of times and
then we collect those photons on a camera.

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:03.480
The ion we're using is ytterbium so it emits
a UV.

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:07.100
If it was a visible color you could actually
see it with your eye, it would be a tiny speck.

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:08.100
Hang on.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:09.570
You can see atoms.

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:10.850
You can see atoms.

