00:32:04.240 --> 00:32:09.690
But let's say you have four cards, right and
you play the game of monte or you might go

00:32:09.690 --> 00:32:10.970
to a street corner somewhere.

00:32:10.970 --> 00:32:13.780
Not that we're advocating you do that.

00:32:13.780 --> 00:32:14.990
Don’t do that.

00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:19.049
And so out of these four cards you've got
one of them which is different, one of them

00:32:19.049 --> 00:32:20.049
is the queen.

00:32:20.049 --> 00:32:26.320
And now, now we're going to flip them over
and when you play this game you're randomly

00:32:26.320 --> 00:32:28.790
going to try to find where that where that
queen is right.

00:32:28.790 --> 00:32:33.350
You're going to try once and you're going
to flip over a card and see whether or not

00:32:33.350 --> 00:32:35.110
it's the queen.

00:32:35.110 --> 00:32:39.170
And so on, on in playing this game you really
only have a one in four chance of getting

00:32:39.170 --> 00:32:42.799
it right on your on your first try.

00:32:42.799 --> 00:32:46.929
But now what's interesting about this type
of game is we can also ask well how would

00:32:46.929 --> 00:32:49.710
we how do we do this if we had a computer?

00:32:49.710 --> 00:32:54.020
What what what does a classic computer do
with this game and what does a quantum computer

00:32:54.020 --> 00:32:55.450
do with this game?

00:32:55.450 --> 00:32:56.450
OK.

00:32:56.450 --> 00:33:02.330
So in the case of a classical processor what
we're doing is when we when we flip over these

00:33:02.330 --> 00:33:06.320
cards you can think of this as as storing
a database.

00:33:06.320 --> 00:33:09.010
In this case we can also call it an oracle.

00:33:09.010 --> 00:33:19.010
So you store the database with the hidden
set of cards where the queen is properly located.

00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:25.370
With a classical computer what you're going
to do is in order to to find where it is you're

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:27.330
going to look at all the possible arrangements.

00:33:27.330 --> 00:33:28.330
Right.

00:33:28.330 --> 00:33:29.850
So you're going to start with one particular
arrangement.

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:33.679
Let's, let's start with placing the queen
in the first slot.

00:33:33.679 --> 00:33:40.540
And we're going to take that entry, use it
as an input, we're going to do some processing,

00:33:40.540 --> 00:33:44.929
in this case the green box where you're gonna
do some comparison with what's in the database

00:33:44.929 --> 00:33:47.720
and you're going to make a decision at the
end of it whether or not it correct or not.

00:33:47.720 --> 00:33:51.090
In this case it was not correct and you get
a zero.

00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:52.090
OK.

00:33:52.090 --> 00:33:54.940
And so now then you can try the next one.

00:33:54.940 --> 00:33:59.850
And again you're going to get to get a zero
and then the next one and this time you get

00:33:59.850 --> 00:34:00.850
it right.

00:34:00.850 --> 00:34:04.710
But of course classically you're going to
go through all four of them.

00:34:04.710 --> 00:34:11.020
And so after you go through all four you see
that on average you would have gotten this

00:34:11.020 --> 00:34:16.869
correct, basically you would get a correct
after querying this database about two and

00:34:16.869 --> 00:34:17.960
a quarter times.

00:34:17.960 --> 00:34:24.159
Right so this problem of search in this case
with a classical computer you can only kind

00:34:24.159 --> 00:34:29.619
of do this sequential, sequentially or by
choosing at random.

00:34:29.619 --> 00:34:34.210
But with a quantum processor, this is where
a lot of the ideas of quantum mechanics can

00:34:34.210 --> 00:34:36.010
come through.

00:34:36.010 --> 00:34:42.929
And so in the next slide here with the quantum
processor you have access to superposition.

00:34:42.929 --> 00:34:47.610
And so just like we talked about how you can
be in zero and one at the same time, what

00:34:47.610 --> 00:34:54.110
you can do with two qubit system is to make
a superposition of all four of the possibilities:

00:34:54.110 --> 00:35:01.680
00, 01, 10, 11, to represent all four of the
possible arrangements of this hidden queen.

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:08.900
And so we can take that superposition state,
use it as as basically as an input, call the

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:11.640
database just once.

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:16.570
Perform some processing step, in this case
the processing step involves entanglement

00:35:16.570 --> 00:35:22.410
and it involves this quantum interference
of adding adding together the waves.

00:35:22.410 --> 00:35:26.910
And it'll amplify the answer for exactly the
right answer.

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:31.140
And so every time, no matter what you what
you place into the database, wherever you

00:35:31.140 --> 00:35:37.040
hid that card, you use only one call to the
database, you get the right answer using this

00:35:37.040 --> 00:35:38.040
algorithm.

00:35:38.040 --> 00:35:39.770
And this particular algorithm is known as
Grover's algorithm.

00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:47.830
It is a simple case that gives you the sense
for what is done differently in terms of processing

00:35:47.830 --> 00:35:51.070
information on a classic computer versus a
quantum computer.

00:35:51.070 --> 00:35:55.930
And so on the next slide what would you actually
would do when you want to program an actual

00:35:55.930 --> 00:36:01.109
quantum, quantum processor is to use this
language of quantum gates.

00:36:01.109 --> 00:36:05.609
And so what you see here is actually a quantum
circuit.

00:36:05.609 --> 00:36:13.220
And as Seth alluded to the idea of music,
we call this actually a score because it kind

00:36:13.220 --> 00:36:15.360
of looks like a musical score.

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:21.730
And the concept of time really in time with
gates really has a strong analogy here because

00:36:21.730 --> 00:36:25.700
it's like you're playing different notes on
these different qubits.

00:36:25.700 --> 00:36:29.550
What you see here is really just, just two
of the qubits being populated with these different

00:36:29.550 --> 00:36:33.200
operations, which realizes Grover's algorithm.

00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:38.250
And to break it down a little bit further
in the next slide what you, what you see is

00:36:38.250 --> 00:36:45.300
that these various steps of superposition,
